1 XFC

   XFC (extended function call, opcode FC) is the VAX architecture's
   opening for instructions a system adds.  A byte after it chooses the
   function.  govax uses it to reach the emulator from VAX code: the
   microkernel's console I/O, the system services, and the run-time
   library routines govax supplies in Go.  The assembler knows each
   function's name.

     XFC$CONSOLE_WRITE   01   Writes R0's low byte to the terminal.
     XFC$CONSOLE_READ    02   Reads a character from the terminal into
                              R0's low byte; the rest of R0 is kept.
     XFC$CONSOLE_CMD     03   Runs the console command in the counted
                              string R0 addresses (a word of length,
                              then the text), as if typed at VAX>.  R0
                              becomes 0 if it succeeded, 1 if not.
     XFC$CONSOLE_PUT     04   Writes the string whose descriptor R0
                              addresses to the terminal, all at once.
                              R0 becomes SS$_NORMAL.
     XFC$JSBSHIM         05   Calls a run-time library routine reached
                              by JSB, its code on the stack.
     XFC$QUIT_EMULATION  78   Ends govax (kernel mode only).
     XFC$P1VECTOR        7A   Calls the system service whose P1 vector
                              entry this is.
     XFC$HALT_SILENT     7B   Halts the CPU without a message.
     XFC$HALT            7C   Halts the CPU.
     XFC$SHIM            7D   Calls the run-time library routine whose
                              code is in R0.
     XFC$VMW             7E   Translates the virtual address in R0 to a
     XFC$VMR             7F   physical one in R0, whether or not memory
                              management is on; PSL<V> is set if the page
                              isn't mapped.

   Any other function is a reserved operand fault.  The debugger's
   SHOW SHIM lists the run-time library routines.
